  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "You're given that January 1st, 2000, was a Saturday. Use congruences to determine the day of the week on November 4th, 2000.\n\n*Hint: The year 2000 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*",
  "worked_solution": "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2000, and November 1st, 2000. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 4th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+29(1)}}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(29)+3 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(1)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡14\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as January 1st, 2000, namely Saturday.",
